{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-126922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/state.json" "file_sha256" "5309709b1ea193a28f9093382172180b60526c0aaf567e20b12a422b0af2078f"} {"question" "q-126922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{5},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/state.json" "file_sha256" "5309709b1ea193a28f9093382172180b60526c0aaf567e20b12a422b0af2078f"} {"question" "q-126922" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "37291c515bc5b75aae1626a4e626f7f836ecad8c363f734ea825e705772440e7"} {"question" "q-126922" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/state.json" "file_sha256" "5309709b1ea193a28f9093382172180b60526c0aaf567e20b12a422b0af2078f"} {"question" "q-126922" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}x \\\\ \\sqrt{5}y\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/state.json" "file_sha256" "5309709b1ea193a28f9093382172180b60526c0aaf567e20b12a422b0af2078f"} {"question" "q-126922" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/state.json" "file_sha256" "5309709b1ea193a28f9093382172180b60526c0aaf567e20b12a422b0af2078f"} {"question" "q-149682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=7.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/state.json" "file_sha256" "5309709b1ea193a28f9093382172180b60526c0aaf567e20b12a422b0af2078f"} {"question" "q-149682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{7},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/state.json" "file_sha256" "5309709b1ea193a28f9093382172180b60526c0aaf567e20b12a422b0af2078f"} {"question" "q-149682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "37291c515bc5b75aae1626a4e626f7f836ecad8c363f734ea825e705772440e7"} {"question" "q-149682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/referen