low three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=5,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052255/state.json" "file_sha256" "5309709b1ea193a28f9093382172180b60526c0aaf567e20b12a422b0af2078f"} {"question" "q-126920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "categor